1
00:00:02,820 --> 00:00:05,100
Hello, everyone, and welcome to EnglishPod.

2
00:00:05,360 --> 00:00:06,360
My name is Marco.

3
00:00:06,360 --> 00:00:11,380
My name is Catherine, and today we've got one more episode in our series about interview skills.

4
00:00:12,040 --> 00:00:17,320
This is an elementary level series, and we're here today to talk about things that you might not be very good at.

5
00:00:17,800 --> 00:00:17,980
That's right.

6
00:00:18,800 --> 00:00:22,340
Today we're going to talk about weaknesses, and that is our word for vocabulary preview.

7
00:00:22,680 --> 00:00:23,540
So let's take a look at it now.

8
00:00:26,280 --> 00:00:26,640
Vocabulary preview.

9
00:00:28,100 --> 00:00:30,000
Okay, so what is a weakness?

10
00:00:30,580 --> 00:00:38,240
A weakness is a thing or an activity or something that you're just not very good at or something that you think you can improve.

11
00:00:38,740 --> 00:00:50,320
Okay, so obviously no one is perfect, and like we saw in the previous lesson, we had a lot of good things about ourselves, key strengths, and now we're going to talk about our weaknesses.

12
00:00:50,620 --> 00:00:52,540
Maybe you're a little bit lazy.

13
00:00:52,800 --> 00:00:53,480
That might be a weakness.

14
00:00:53,900 --> 00:00:54,240
That's right.

15
00:00:54,840 --> 00:01:02,080
A weakness is the opposite of strength, and that is the topic today, so let's take a listen to today's dialogue.

16
00:01:06,730 --> 00:01:07,530
Okay, Rebecca.

17
00:01:08,080 --> 00:01:14,230
Well I think you've given me a clear impression of your positive qualities, but let's talk a little bit about your weaknesses.

18
00:01:14,750 --> 00:01:15,110
Okay.

19
00:01:15,470 --> 00:01:17,990
Well it's always more difficult to describe them, isn't it?

20
00:01:18,350 --> 00:01:21,830
Definitely, but if you had to pinpoint one weakness, what would it be?

21
00:01:22,410 --> 00:01:27,930
Well as I mentioned before, I do tend to get frustrated if I don't see progress in my work or career.

22
00:01:28,390 --> 00:01:30,470
I suppose I'm quite a restless character.

23
00:01:31,270 --> 00:01:34,050
My father always taught me to be a high achiever, so...

24
00:01:34,050 --> 00:01:37,850
So would you say if things don't go your way at work, it could easily get you down?

25
00:01:38,210 --> 00:01:45,510
Well in a way, yes, but I must say that even if I'm not completely happy in my work, I always give 110%.

26
00:01:45,510 --> 00:01:47,390
I would never shirk my responsibilities.

27
00:01:48,290 --> 00:01:50,790
I suppose sometimes I expect too much too soon.

28
00:01:51,540 --> 00:01:57,760
Well you know journalism is a highly competitive world, so you do need to keep pushing yourself, it's true.

29
00:01:58,400 --> 00:02:01,980
Okay, well let's move on to talk about the job position here, shall we?

30
00:02:02,220 --> 00:02:02,820
Yes, please.

31
00:02:03,440 --> 00:02:06,600
Alright, we're back.

32
00:02:06,600 --> 00:02:14,800
So obviously, Rebecca did a very good job at talking about her weaknesses in a very positive way.

33
00:02:14,800 --> 00:02:19,800
So before we get into analyzing that, why don't we take a look at a couple of words she used on Language Takeaway.

34
00:02:23,400 --> 00:02:23,820
Language Takeaway.

35
00:02:25,440 --> 00:02:30,090
One of the first words that Mr. Parsons used was a clear impression, so impression.

36
00:02:31,120 --> 00:02:37,450
Alright so I think this is a very common word when people talk about first impressions or I get the impression.

37
00:02:37,840 --> 00:02:39,440
So what is an impression?

38
00:02:39,440 --> 00:02:42,880
Well an impression is like an idea or a feeling.

39
00:02:43,480 --> 00:02:51,940
So I can have an impression about something, it means that I don't necessarily understand it really well or I don't have experience with it, but it's an idea that I have about something.

40
00:02:52,360 --> 00:02:59,020
That's right, so for example, maybe you meet somebody new and you say I get the impression that he is a liar.

41
00:02:59,740 --> 00:03:01,520
But maybe then you get to know him and he's not.

42
00:03:01,820 --> 00:03:06,240
So this is your idea about something or the way you feel about something.

43
00:03:06,480 --> 00:03:11,520
So in this case, Mr. Parsons has a clear impression of her positive qualities.

44
00:03:11,520 --> 00:03:18,440
That means that he has an idea or an understanding of the things that Rebecca is good at, but he wants to know more about her weaknesses.

45
00:03:18,980 --> 00:03:24,940
That's right and Mr. Parsons asked her to try and pinpoint one of her weaknesses.

46
00:03:25,340 --> 00:03:26,440
So this word to pinpoint.

47
00:03:27,100 --> 00:03:33,320
This is a great word, I'm not really sure where it comes from in English, but I think it's very visual, you can see what the word means.

48
00:03:34,760 --> 00:03:41,180
Pinpoint means to locate or identify or really be specific about something.

49
00:03:41,760 --> 00:03:51,120
That's right, so maybe if you go to the doctor and you say that your back hurts, the doctor might ask you to try and pinpoint where on your back you have the pain.

50
00:03:51,880 --> 00:03:56,560
That's right, so it's a way to be specific or very exact about something.

51
00:03:56,880 --> 00:04:02,640
Okay, so if she had to pinpoint something, she said, well, I do get a little bit frustrated.

52
00:04:03,180 --> 00:04:05,900
Okay, so frustrated is a way you feel.

53
00:04:06,280 --> 00:04:08,140
I feel frustrated, he is frustrated.

54
00:04:08,900 --> 00:04:17,770
It's basically a feeling that you get when you have to do something, but you don't think you can do it or maybe something annoys you and you get frustrated.

55
00:04:18,810 --> 00:04:26,620
For example, when people tap like this and I'm working, I get frustrated because it's a distraction.

56
00:04:27,080 --> 00:04:29,960
Okay, so everyone has different things that frustrate them.

57
00:04:29,960 --> 00:04:38,820
Now if we take a look at this word, it is actually an adjective and we usually use it, you get frustrated or as you said, you feel frustrated.

58
00:04:39,520 --> 00:04:45,780
So remember, it may look like a verb, but it's actually an adjective that ends in ed, frustrated, to get frustrated.

59
00:04:46,280 --> 00:04:49,140
He got frustrated or we feel very frustrated.

60
00:04:49,460 --> 00:04:56,500
Okay, and well, apart from being frustrated, she also said that she is a restless character.

61
00:04:57,180 --> 00:05:02,500
Okay, so someone is restless if they don't like to stay in one place all the time.

62
00:05:02,720 --> 00:05:08,640
That means someone who wants to move around or to go places or to travel or to see new things is restless.

63
00:05:09,320 --> 00:05:09,800
That's right.

64
00:05:10,060 --> 00:05:13,600
So she has a restless character, that means she likes to move around.

65
00:05:13,820 --> 00:05:16,340
She doesn't like to stand still in one place.

66
00:05:16,560 --> 00:05:18,360
Maybe she gets bored very easily.

67
00:05:18,860 --> 00:05:21,200
Okay, so that's somebody that would be restless.

68
00:05:22,080 --> 00:05:28,420
But moving on, she also said that she doesn't like to shirk her responsibilities.

69
00:05:29,260 --> 00:05:31,160
Okay, you often hear these words go together.

70
00:05:31,440 --> 00:05:35,600
So responsibilities are things that we need to do, right?

71
00:05:35,600 --> 00:05:44,030
Things that other people expect us to do, to shirk a responsibility means to not do something that you're supposed to do.

72
00:05:44,200 --> 00:05:48,640
It means to avoid responsibility or to push it away.

73
00:05:48,640 --> 00:05:52,030
Okay, so it's a verb, to shirk your responsibilities.

74
00:05:52,440 --> 00:05:54,520
You shouldn't shirk your responsibilities.

75
00:05:54,520 --> 00:05:54,650
That's right.

76
00:05:55,520 --> 00:05:57,720
Alright, so a lot of interesting key words there.

77
00:05:57,730 --> 00:06:01,130
Why don't we listen to the dialogue again and we'll be back with Fluency Builder.

78
00:06:05,560 --> 00:06:12,960
Okay Rebecca, well I think you've given me a clear impression of your positive qualities, but let's talk a little bit about your weaknesses.

79
00:06:13,520 --> 00:06:16,740
Okay, well it's always more difficult to describe them, isn't it?

80
00:06:17,120 --> 00:06:20,600
Definitely, but if you had to pinpoint one weakness, what would it be?

81
00:06:21,160 --> 00:06:26,720
Well as I mentioned before, I do tend to get frustrated if I don't see progress in my work or career.

82
00:06:27,340 --> 00:06:29,300
I suppose I'm quite a restless character.

83
00:06:29,940 --> 00:06:33,020
My father always taught me to be a high achiever, so...

84
00:06:33,050 --> 00:06:37,050
So would you say if things don't go your way at work, it could easily get you down?

85
00:06:37,510 --> 00:06:38,990
Well, in a way, yes.

86
00:06:39,470 --> 00:06:44,730
But I must say that even if I'm not completely happy in my work, I always give 110%.

87
00:06:44,730 --> 00:06:46,590
I would never shirk my responsibilities.

88
00:06:47,570 --> 00:06:50,070
I suppose sometimes I expect too much too soon.

89
00:06:50,750 --> 00:06:56,950
Well, you know journalism is a highly competitive world, so you do need to keep pushing yourself, it's true.

90
00:06:57,690 --> 00:07:01,190
Okay, well let's move on to talk about the job position here, shall we?

91
00:07:01,350 --> 00:07:01,950
Yes please.

92
00:07:07,120 --> 00:07:10,020
So we've picked out three key phrases for you on Fluency Builder.

93
00:07:10,280 --> 00:07:11,400
Let's take a look at those now.

94
00:07:11,980 --> 00:07:20,420
Starting with Mr. Parson saying, so would you say if things don't go your way at work, it could easily get you down?

95
00:07:20,880 --> 00:07:22,020
Two great phrases there.

96
00:07:22,300 --> 00:07:22,660
That's right.

97
00:07:23,540 --> 00:07:26,120
So the first phrase, if things don't go your way.

98
00:07:26,120 --> 00:07:32,990
So this is kind of a funny phrase when you think about it because they go your way like to me or what is my way.

99
00:07:33,400 --> 00:07:40,090
This basically means if things do not happen in a way that makes you happy or in a way that you expect.

100
00:07:40,750 --> 00:07:47,510
Okay, so we could say he lost his job, he couldn't pay his bills, things just didn't go his way.

101
00:07:47,730 --> 00:07:48,130
That's right.

102
00:07:48,920 --> 00:07:54,220
So things are not happening as he planned or as he expected.

103
00:07:54,220 --> 00:07:55,080
You can also say the opposite.

104
00:07:55,480 --> 00:08:01,840
So he won the lottery and he also met a really beautiful girl who wanted to be his girlfriend.

105
00:08:01,840 --> 00:08:03,060
Things were going his way.

106
00:08:03,360 --> 00:08:04,280
Okay, very good.

107
00:08:04,500 --> 00:08:06,800
So if things are going your way, it's good.

108
00:08:07,140 --> 00:08:09,420
If things aren't going your way, then it's bad.

109
00:08:09,600 --> 00:08:09,880
That's right.

110
00:08:10,200 --> 00:08:17,200
Okay, and so if things aren't going Rebecca's way, does that mean that it could easily get her down?

111
00:08:17,720 --> 00:08:25,520
Okay, so to get you down or to get someone down means to make you sad, upset or frustrated like that where we just heard.

112
00:08:25,560 --> 00:08:29,220
So you could ask someone like, Marco, what gets you down?

113
00:08:29,860 --> 00:08:30,520
Rainy days.

114
00:08:31,020 --> 00:08:31,600
That's a good one.

115
00:08:32,240 --> 00:08:33,720
Yeah, rainy days get me down too.

116
00:08:33,720 --> 00:08:38,320
So it's kind of a way of saying what makes you sad or depressed or what makes you unhappy.

117
00:08:38,960 --> 00:08:44,800
Or in this case, if something doesn't happen as you planned it, if it doesn't go your way, does that make you sad?

118
00:08:45,200 --> 00:08:46,120
Does that get you down?

119
00:08:46,720 --> 00:08:48,000
Okay, very good.

120
00:08:48,280 --> 00:08:49,680
So a very good question there.

121
00:08:50,280 --> 00:08:57,060
And she responded that kind of, yeah, that does happen, but she always tries to give 110%.

122
00:08:57,560 --> 00:09:02,100
Okay, so this is a really funny phrase because you think I can give everything I have.

123
00:09:02,280 --> 00:09:04,380
I can work really hard and that's 100%.

124
00:09:04,840 --> 00:09:08,280
But we say 110% means you do more than you can.

125
00:09:08,880 --> 00:09:11,200
It's like pushing yourself more than is possible.

126
00:09:12,000 --> 00:09:12,180
That's right.

127
00:09:13,000 --> 00:09:16,540
So this is a very common phrase in sports and business.

128
00:09:16,540 --> 00:09:22,860
People are saying, for example, I always remember my coach saying, I want you guys to give me 110% out there.

129
00:09:23,320 --> 00:09:28,250
That means I really want you to try very hard to do as much as possible.

130
00:09:28,720 --> 00:09:30,890
Even more than your best, more than you think you can do.

131
00:09:31,480 --> 00:09:32,480
Exactly.

132
00:09:32,480 --> 00:09:35,200
So again, it refers to the effort that you put into something.

133
00:09:35,200 --> 00:09:36,870
It can be in business, sports, anything.

134
00:09:37,390 --> 00:09:40,470
And she's basically saying, I always do more than my best.

135
00:09:40,890 --> 00:09:42,610
I do as much as humanly possible.

136
00:09:43,050 --> 00:09:43,490
That's right.

137
00:09:44,280 --> 00:09:46,050
Okay, so a lot of great stuff there from Rebecca.

138
00:09:46,480 --> 00:09:49,870
Why don't we listen to her one last time and we'll be back in a bit.

139
00:09:57,210 --> 00:09:57,950
Okay, Rebecca.

140
00:09:58,560 --> 00:10:02,170
Well I think you've given me a clear impression of your positive qualities.

141
00:10:02,840 --> 00:10:04,630
But let's talk a little bit about your weaknesses.

142
00:10:05,200 --> 00:10:05,530
Okay.

143
00:10:05,910 --> 00:10:08,390
Well it's always more difficult to describe them, isn't it?

144
00:10:08,770 --> 00:10:09,210
Definitely.

145
00:10:09,830 --> 00:10:12,250
But if you had to pinpoint one weakness, what would it be?

146
00:10:12,830 --> 00:10:18,370
Well as I mentioned before, I do tend to get frustrated if I don't see progress in my work or career.

147
00:10:18,830 --> 00:10:20,910
I suppose I'm quite a restless character.

148
00:10:21,710 --> 00:10:24,510
My father always taught me to be a high achiever, so...

149
00:10:24,510 --> 00:10:28,270
So would you say if things don't go your way at work, it could easily get you down?

150
00:10:28,630 --> 00:10:30,210
Well in a way, yes.

151
00:10:30,690 --> 00:10:35,930
But I must say that even if I'm not completely happy in my work, I always give 110%.

152
00:10:35,930 --> 00:10:37,830
I would never shirk my responsibilities.

153
00:10:38,710 --> 00:10:41,250
I suppose sometimes I expect too much too soon.

154
00:10:41,960 --> 00:10:48,180
Well you know journalism is a highly competitive world, so you do need to keep pushing yourself, it's true.

155
00:10:48,870 --> 00:10:52,410
Okay, well let's move on to talk about the job position here, shall we?

156
00:10:52,650 --> 00:10:53,330
Yes, please.

157
00:10:59,650 --> 00:11:07,830
Alright, so talking about weaknesses, I think one of the common mistakes when people talk about weaknesses is they say they work too much.

158
00:11:08,120 --> 00:11:10,390
And everyone says you should never say that in an interview.

159
00:11:10,720 --> 00:11:19,190
Yeah, I think it's a bad way to talk about yourself because a boss or an employer really wants to hear a real weakness.

160
00:11:20,120 --> 00:11:23,520
And I work too hard is a way of saying, I have no weaknesses.

161
00:11:24,180 --> 00:11:25,400
I'm actually really, really perfect.

162
00:11:26,120 --> 00:11:27,680
I just think it's a lazy answer.

163
00:11:27,920 --> 00:11:34,920
So maybe you should avoid saying I work too hard and really focus on things that you do have to work on.

164
00:11:34,920 --> 00:11:42,140
And then I think the key is to say that I know this is something that I have a hard time with, I know this is a weakness and I'm trying to improve it.

165
00:11:42,560 --> 00:11:51,400
Yeah, I remember in some of the interviews I've had, I think my common answer was sometimes I'm very late, I'm not very punctual.

166
00:11:53,440 --> 00:12:12,000
But you know, it's a real weakness and it may seem like difficult, but then you give it a little spin by saying, yeah, I'm sometimes not very punctual, but then I try to make up for it, maybe staying more time at the office, or sometimes I try to wake up a little bit earlier and make it on time.

167
00:12:12,000 --> 00:12:13,000
I don't know, something like that.

168
00:12:13,000 --> 00:12:14,000
Right.

169
00:12:14,000 --> 00:12:17,440
So the key there is just to show that you know it's a problem and that you're trying to work on it.

170
00:12:17,440 --> 00:12:18,440
Yeah.

171
00:12:18,440 --> 00:12:24,960
So this is actually the hardest part for me of an interview, talking about yourself in a negative way, because obviously you want to get the job.

172
00:12:25,120 --> 00:12:29,820
So you want to make sure that the employer has a good idea of yourself, but it's hard.

173
00:12:30,060 --> 00:12:31,300
I think it's the hardest part for everyone.

174
00:12:31,540 --> 00:12:32,600
I don't think it's just you.

175
00:12:32,800 --> 00:12:33,380
Yeah, it's hard.

176
00:12:33,760 --> 00:12:35,120
So this is an interesting topic.

177
00:12:35,380 --> 00:12:42,780
Why don't you practice and come to our website EnglishPod.com and on our comment section, why don't you tell us what your weaknesses are?

178
00:12:43,250 --> 00:12:43,610
That's right.

179
00:12:43,750 --> 00:12:44,490
We hope to hear from you.

180
00:12:44,650 --> 00:12:48,050
Of course, you can also share with us your strengths on our lesson about strengths.

181
00:12:48,610 --> 00:12:53,880
But in this one, I recommend everyone practice this because it will come up at some point and it's good to get the practice in.

182
00:12:54,090 --> 00:12:55,950
So EnglishPod.com, we hope to see you there.

183
00:12:56,090 --> 00:12:56,430
All right, guys.

184
